= The Hamburg uprising

At five in the morning on 23 October, Hamburg's party district, which has the Left on its committees, acts on the earlier order and attacks the police stations of the harbour districts. About three hundred fighters, among the leaders Hugo Urbahns and Hans Kippenberger, attack seventeen police stations and take some in Barmbek and Schiffbek. They are on their own: the workers had not been properly told that the rising was cancelled, and Hamburg was the only place where the party fought.

Within two days the Hamburg police end it. About a hundred are dead: some twenty insurgents, seventeen policemen, and about sixty bystanders. It is not a revolution, and it is not nothing: it shows that the best fighters of the party followed a leadership that did not tell them the plan was changed.

